1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,037
Mission log, day 212. Mars, the year 2140.

2
00:00:05,537 --> 00:00:07,116
Our rover's map is blank.

3
00:00:07,516 --> 00:00:12,700
There's no GPS out here. No satellites
overhead, quietly announcing the time.

4
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:14,749
So before we drive anywhere,

5
00:00:14,749 --> 00:00:18,752
we have to rebuild a trick that every
phone back on Earth pulls off,

6
00:00:18,752 --> 00:00:20,460
every second it's navigating.

7
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:23,640
Here's how that trick works, and
why it almost didn't.

8
00:00:28,040 --> 00:00:30,977
Right now, about 20,000 km
above your head,

9
00:00:30,977 --> 00:00:35,827
a few dozen satellites are doing one
simple thing: announcing the time.

10
00:00:36,227 --> 00:00:38,979
Your phone never answers
them. It only listens.

11
00:00:39,279 --> 00:00:42,985
And from those announcements alone,
it works out where you're standing,

12
00:00:42,985 --> 00:00:44,186
to within a few metres.

13
00:00:44,686 --> 00:00:49,061
But if the engineers who built this
system had ignored Albert Einstein,

14
00:00:49,061 --> 00:00:53,497
that blue dot would drift away from you
by kilometres, every single day.

15
00:00:53,997 --> 00:00:55,485
In the next few minutes,

16
00:00:55,485 --> 00:01:00,134
you'll see how a handful of clock
readings from space become that blue dot,

17
00:01:00,134 --> 00:01:03,234
and why GPS only works
because Einstein was right.

18
00:01:04,234 --> 00:01:06,303
Let's start with a trick you already know.

19
00:01:06,603 --> 00:01:10,486
When you see lightning, you can count
the seconds until the thunder arrives.

20
00:01:10,786 --> 00:01:13,352
Light reaches you almost instantly,

21
00:01:13,352 --> 00:01:17,165
but sound crawls along
at about 340 metres a second.

22
00:01:17,465 --> 00:01:22,137
So every three seconds of waiting means the
storm is roughly a kilometre away.

23
00:01:22,537 --> 00:01:27,297
You've just turned time into distance.
GPS does exactly the same thing,

24
00:01:27,297 --> 00:01:29,577
with radio waves instead of sound.

25
00:01:30,077 --> 00:01:33,587
There are more than 30
working GPS satellites,

26
00:01:33,587 --> 00:01:39,997
spread across six orbits about 20,200 km up.
Each one circles the Earth twice a day.

27
00:01:40,397 --> 00:01:44,756
And each one constantly broadcasts
a message that says, in effect:

28
00:01:44,756 --> 00:01:48,322
"I am satellite number 12. This
is exactly where I am.

29
00:01:48,322 --> 00:01:50,765
And this is exactly what time it is."

30
00:01:51,165 --> 00:01:56,776
Radio waves travel at the speed of light,
about 300,000 km every second.

31
00:01:57,076 --> 00:01:59,290
From a satellite straight overhead,

32
00:01:59,290 --> 00:02:02,452
the message takes about
0.07 seconds to reach you.

33
00:02:02,752 --> 00:02:07,193
Your phone compares the time stamped
in the message with the moment it arrived,

34
00:02:07,193 --> 00:02:11,072
multiplies the difference by the speed
of light, and gets a distance.

35
00:02:11,472 --> 00:02:15,304
That's the first big idea. GPS
doesn't measure where you are.

36
00:02:15,304 --> 00:02:18,320
It measures how long
a signal took to reach you.

37
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:21,944
But one distance on its
own isn't much use.

38
00:02:22,244 --> 00:02:26,172
If a friend tells you they're exactly 5
km from the railway station,

39
00:02:26,172 --> 00:02:28,772
they could be anywhere
on a circle around it.

40
00:02:29,172 --> 00:02:31,973
In space, it's the same idea in 3D.

41
00:02:31,973 --> 00:02:38,617
Knowing you're 21,000 km from one satellite
puts you somewhere on a giant imaginary

42
00:02:38,617 --> 00:02:39,177
sphere.

43
00:02:39,477 --> 00:02:43,680
A second satellite narrows that down
to the circle where the two spheres meet.

44
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:46,661
A third cuts that circle to
just two points,

45
00:02:46,661 --> 00:02:51,169
and one of them is usually far out in
space, so your phone throws it away.

46
00:02:51,469 --> 00:02:55,501
Three satellites, one location.
This is called trilateration.

47
00:02:56,101 --> 00:02:58,725
So why does your phone
actually need at least four?

48
00:02:59,225 --> 00:03:04,033
Because the weak link is in your pocket.
The satellites carry atomic clocks.

49
00:03:04,033 --> 00:03:08,462
Your phone has a cheap quartz clock, the
same kind as in a wristwatch.

50
00:03:08,862 --> 00:03:13,679
So your phone treats its own clock
error as one more unknown. Four unknowns:

51
00:03:13,679 --> 00:03:17,545
east–west, north–south, up–down,
and "how wrong is my clock?"

52
00:03:17,945 --> 00:03:20,258
Four unknowns need four measurements.

53
00:03:20,258 --> 00:03:24,695
The fourth satellite turns your phone's
cheap clock into a precise one,

54
00:03:24,695 --> 00:03:25,945
borrowed from space.

55
00:03:26,945 --> 00:03:31,041
Why does the clock matter so much?
Because light is ridiculously fast.

56
00:03:31,341 --> 00:03:36,475
In one nanosecond, a billionth of a second,
light travels about 30 centimetres.

57
00:03:36,475 --> 00:03:38,424
Roughly the length of a ruler.

58
00:03:38,824 --> 00:03:42,880
So a clock that's off by one
microsecond, a millionth of a second,

59
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:47,613
puts you about 300 metres off. That's
your front door versus the next street.

60
00:03:48,013 --> 00:03:51,902
That's why every GPS satellite
carries several atomic clocks.

61
00:03:51,902 --> 00:03:55,408
They count the vibrations of
atoms, which never change,

62
00:03:55,408 --> 00:03:59,042
and stay accurate to within
a few billionths of a second.

63
00:03:59,442 --> 00:04:04,215
Ground stations check those clocks, track
every orbit, and upload corrections,

64
00:04:04,215 --> 00:04:08,743
so your phone always knows exactly where
each satellite was when it spoke.

65
00:04:09,143 --> 00:04:14,394
Put it all together and a phone can find
itself to within about 5 metres under open

66
00:04:14,394 --> 00:04:14,647
sky.

67
00:04:14,947 --> 00:04:18,741
Near tall buildings, signals
bounce off walls and arrive late.

68
00:04:18,741 --> 00:04:22,840
That's why your blue dot sometimes
jumps across the road in a city.

69
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:27,450
But there's one more problem. And no
amount of engineering could fix it,

70
00:04:27,450 --> 00:04:31,788
because it isn't a problem with the
clocks. It's a problem with time itself.

71
00:04:32,988 --> 00:04:34,213
In 1905 and 1915,

72
00:04:34,213 --> 00:04:39,687
Albert Einstein showed that time doesn't
tick at the same rate for everyone.

73
00:04:39,987 --> 00:04:45,299
GPS satellites are caught between two of his
ideas, pulling in opposite directions.

74
00:04:45,699 --> 00:04:49,091
First, special relativity:
moving clocks run slow.

75
00:04:49,391 --> 00:04:54,970
GPS satellites race around the Earth at
nearly 4 km every second. At that speed,

76
00:04:54,970 --> 00:05:00,271
their clocks fall behind clocks on the
ground by about 7 microseconds a day.

77
00:05:00,671 --> 00:05:03,652
Second, general relativity:
gravity slows time.

78
00:05:03,652 --> 00:05:07,839
The deeper you sit in a gravity
well, the slower your clock ticks.

79
00:05:08,139 --> 00:05:11,883
The satellites sit far higher
in Earth's well than you do,

80
00:05:11,883 --> 00:05:15,691
so their clocks run faster, by
about 45 microseconds a day.

81
00:05:16,091 --> 00:05:23,024
Minus 7, plus 45. Every GPS satellite
clock gains about 38 microseconds a day.

82
00:05:23,524 --> 00:05:27,912
That sounds tiny. But one
microsecond is 300 metres.

83
00:05:27,912 --> 00:05:33,060
38 microseconds is more than
11 km worth of error. Every day.

84
00:05:33,660 --> 00:05:39,307
So engineers built Einstein into
the hardware. Before launch,

85
00:05:39,307 --> 00:05:44,307
each satellite's clock is tuned
to tick slightly slow:

86
00:05:44,307 --> 00:05:48,380
10.22999999543 MHz instead
of exactly 10.23.

87
00:05:48,780 --> 00:05:52,990
Once it's in orbit, relativity speeds it
up to exactly the right rate,

88
00:05:52,990 --> 00:05:54,433
as seen from the ground.

89
00:05:54,933 --> 00:05:57,576
Not everyone was convinced at first.

90
00:05:57,576 --> 00:06:02,496
When the first GPS satellite with a
caesium clock launched in 1977,

91
00:06:02,496 --> 00:06:06,901
its relativity correction was left
switched off for 20 days.

92
00:06:07,301 --> 00:06:11,710
Its clock drifted by just the amount
Einstein's equations predicted.

93
00:06:11,710 --> 00:06:13,720
The correction was switched on,

94
00:06:13,720 --> 00:06:17,157
and it has been built into
every GPS satellite since.

95
00:06:18,357 --> 00:06:22,922
So, how does GPS know where you are? It
doesn't. Your phone works it out.

96
00:06:23,322 --> 00:06:28,154
It listens to at least four satellites,
turns their time stamps into distances,

97
00:06:28,154 --> 00:06:29,683
solves for its own clock,

98
00:06:29,683 --> 00:06:34,393
and trusts atomic clocks that were set for
Einstein before they ever left the

99
00:06:34,393 --> 00:06:34,821
ground.

100
00:06:35,321 --> 00:06:40,322
And most phones don't only listen
to GPS. They also use Europe's Galileo,

101
00:06:40,322 --> 00:06:46,009
Russia's GLONASS, China's BeiDou, and
many add India's NavIC. Different satellites,

102
00:06:46,009 --> 00:06:48,270
same idea: time becomes distance.

103
00:06:49,270 --> 00:06:51,659
Mission log, day 213.

104
00:06:52,059 --> 00:06:56,238
We've drafted a plan: put a few
atomic clocks into orbit around Mars,

105
00:06:56,238 --> 00:07:01,083
and tune them before launch for Mars's
gravity and their own speed, not Earth's.

106
00:07:01,483 --> 00:07:04,662
If Einstein was right on Earth,
he'll be right out here too.

107
00:07:05,862 --> 00:07:09,873
If this changed the way you see that
little blue dot, hit the like button.

108
00:07:10,173 --> 00:07:12,313
Then subscribe to Cogs & Cosmos,

109
00:07:12,313 --> 00:07:17,128
and tap the bell icon so you never
miss a new explainer. Keep exploring.

